1. *vibhattiyo* — [variant reading]: meaning.

10. The pāṭidesanīya rules are reckoned as five;
three are said to be four for the bhikkhus — these are the training rules recited above at the observance days.

11. Forty [rules] of the bhikkhus and bhikkhunīs are not held in common;
of these, [so many] are alike in wording.

12. Two rules to be confessed, with [certain] exceptions, are such;
the nissaggiya rules, twelve in number, are declared in the Vinaya.

13. Thus four pāṭidesanīya rules are known;
forty-four are the rules not shared in common between bhikkhus and bhikkhunīs.

14. Thirty, moreover, of the bhikkhunīs are not shared in common with the bhikkhus;
these are alike in wording.

15. Four pārājika, ten saṅghādisesa, nissaggiya [rules] –
of these, four are pāṭidesanīya as well;
thirty-three are the rules not shared in common between bhikkhus and bhikkhunīs.

16. Seven hundred... only these are held to be "not in common";
these are alike in wording.

17. Four pārājika, sixteen saṅghādisesa are reckoned,
together with two aniyata and twenty-four nissaggiya.

18. Four pāṭidesanīya arising from dependent conditions, together with twelve [others] –
seventy... only these are held to be "not in common."

19. Seven hundred and four [rules] are held in common;
these are alike in wording.

20. Four pārājika, seven saṅghādisesa are reckoned,
forty nissaggiya, twelve... [and] five are reckoned as the pāṭidesanīya;
four hundred and seventy are held in common.

21. The pārājika [offenses] alone, from the beginning, were laid down in due time;
whoever has gone forth and been fully admitted, whether man or woman, is bound by them;
thus the Teacher's Dispensation stands firm.

22. Two are the saṅghādisesa, two the aniyata, forty are the nissaggiya,
[and] four [are] the pāṭinakkhiya, five the pāṭidesanīya.

23. Five are reckoned as [belonging to] the training; with three they are agreed to be settled;
by agreement and by examination, in three ways they are resolved.

24. Two uposathas, two pavāraṇās, four acts — thus the Teacher has taught;
above, four are declared to belong to oneself;
and seven are the stages of offenses.

25. Four are the legal questions; with seven they are agreed to be settled;
with two, with four, with three, thus the matter is settled.

1. "two, with certain exceptions" — so in some [readings].
2. "these are alike in wording" — comparative [reading], so in some [readings].

**ORIGINATION (SAMUṬṬHĀNA)**

There is an offense that arises without volition and ceases with volition;
there is an offense that arises with volition and ceases without volition;
there is an offense that arises without volition and ceases without volition;
there is an offense that arises with volition and ceases with volition;
there is an offense that arises with a wholesome mind and ceases with a wholesome mind;
there is an offense that arises with a wholesome mind and ceases with an unwholesome mind;
there is an offense that arises with a wholesome mind and ceases with an indeterminate mind;
there is an offense that arises with an unwholesome mind and ceases with an unwholesome mind;
there is an offense that arises with an unwholesome mind and ceases with a wholesome mind;
there is an offense that arises with an unwholesome mind and ceases with an indeterminate mind;
there is an offense that arises with an indeterminate mind and ceases with a wholesome mind;
there is an offense that arises with an indeterminate mind and ceases with an unwholesome mind;
there is an offense that arises with an indeterminate mind and ceases with an indeterminate mind.

The first Pārājika originates by bodily origination: the first Pārājika originates by a single origination — it originates from body and from mind, not from speech.

The second Pārājika originates by bodily origination: the second Pārājika originates by three originations — it may originate from body and from mind, not from speech; it may originate from speech and from mind, not from body; it may originate from body, from speech, and from mind.

The third Pārājika originates by bodily origination: the third Pārājika originates by three originations — it may originate from body and from mind, not from speech; it may originate from speech [and from mind], not from body; it may originate from body, from speech, and from mind.

The fourth Pārājika originates by bodily origination: the fourth Pārājika originates by three originations — it may originate from body and from mind, not from speech; it may originate from speech and from mind, not from body; it may originate from body, from speech, and from mind.

The section on the four Pārājikas is concluded.

1. For one who, having first approached, without [prior restraint, commits an offense from the outset] — such an offense originates by bodily origination: it originates by a single origination — it originates from body and from mind, not from speech.

